Hello Puffin Users,

Welcome to the June 2026 edition of "This Month in Open Source."
We've got a packed newsletter for you this time around.

--- LINUX KERNEL 6.8 RELEASED ---

Linus Torvalds announced the release of Linux kernel 6.8 this past
weekend. The release includes several major improvements:

  * Bcachefs filesystem is now considered stable.
  * Improved scheduler performance for hybrid CPU architectures.
  * Better support for the RISC-V vector extension.
  * New AMDGPU power management features.

The merge window for 6.9 is already open, and we're expecting some
interesting networking changes.

--- RUST IN GNOME ---

The GNOME project has been making steady progress on its Rust
bindings. The latest gtk4-rs release now supports:

  * Full GTK4 widget hierarchy bindings
  * Improved documentation with more examples
  * Better cross-compilation support
  * Asynchronous operation helpers

Several core GNOME apps are now experimenting with Rust ports, and
the feedback has been largely positive. The memory safety guarantees
that Rust provides are especially valuable in a large, complex
codebase like GNOME.

--- GO 1.24 ITERATORS ---

The Go team has been working on iterators as a first-class concept
in the language. The design draft proposes a simple interface-based
approach that would allow any type to be iterated over using a
`for range` loop. This would be a significant ergonomic improvement
for working with custom collections.

--- PUFFIN DEVELOPMENT UPDATE ---

In local client news, Puffin continues to make progress on its
IMAP sync engine. Version 0.1.0 is expected to feature:

  * Full offline-first email storage in SQLite
  * Markdown composer with live preview
  * Vim-style keyboard navigation
  * Fuzzy search across all your emails

The team is currently working on getting the IMAP IDLE support
working correctly for push notifications.

--- COMMUNITY HIGHLIGHTS ---

This month we'd like to highlight the work being done on the
"Tide" email client for Sailfish OS. It's always exciting to see
new email clients embracing local-first architecture.

If you're working on an email client or a mail-related open source
project, we'd love to hear from you. Drop us a line and we might
feature your work in next month's newsletter.

